<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-permutations-i" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Permutations I</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>3.0 Statistics and Probability</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>3.1 Permutations and Combinations</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>Perform permutations involving real life situations</p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>11</p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>40 minutes</p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
    <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery: Choosing Leaders</title>
    <dl>
        <li>
            <title>Suggested Time</title>
            <p>15 minutes</p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <title>Objective</title>
            <p>
                To enable learners to discover that when positions or roles are different, the order of selection matters and affects the total number of possible arrangements.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <title>Teacher's Role</title>
            <p>
                Facilitate the leadership committee activity and encourage learners to list or model several possible outcomes. Guide learners to compare outcomes such as "Amina as Prefect and Brian as Assistant Prefect" with "Brian as Prefect and Amina as Assistant Prefect." Ask probing questions such as, "Are these two outcomes the same?" and "Why does the number of choices decrease after each position is filled?" Help learners recognize that different positions create ordered arrangements and encourage them to express the pattern using multiplication before introducing permutation notation.
            </p>
        </li>
    </dl>
</axiom>

    <activity>
        <title>The Leadership Committee</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                In a class of 10 students, you need to elect a <term>prefect</term> and a <term>assistant prefect</term>.
                <ol>
                    <li>How many different students could be chosen as Prefect?</li>
                    <li>Once a Prefect is chosen, how many students are left to choose from for Assistant Prefect?</li>
                    <li>Multiply these choices together. How many total ways can this leadership pair be formed?</li>
                    <li>If we were just picking two "helpers" with no titles, would the answer be the same? Why or why not?</li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </activity>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
    <title>Structured Instruction</title>
    <p>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>10 minutes</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To formalize learners' understanding of ordered arrangements and introduce the concept and formula for permutations.
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </p>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    <alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert>
                    Learners observed that selecting a Prefect and an Assistant Prefect involves filling different positions. Changing the order of the same people produces a different outcome.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    <alert>When Order Matters:</alert>
                    Explain that arrangements in which position or order is important are called <term>permutations</term>. Examples include leadership positions, race results, passwords, seating arrangements, and queues.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    <alert>The Permutation Formula:</alert>
                    If <m>r</m> objects are selected and arranged from a set of <m>n</m> distinct objects, the number of permutations is:
                    <me>_nP_r=\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}</me>
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    <alert>Understanding the Formula:</alert>
                    The numerator represents the decreasing choices available at each stage of selection, while the denominator removes unnecessary factors after <m>r</m> positions have been filled.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    <alert>Efficient Evaluation:</alert>
                    Encourage learners to simplify factorial expressions by expanding only as far as necessary. For example:
                    <me>_8P_3=\frac{8!}{5!}=8 \times 7 \times 6=336</me>
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </paragraphs>
</axiom>

    <insight>
        <title>Permutations: When Order Matters</title>
        <p>
            A <term>permutation</term> is an arrangement of items where the <term>order</term> is important. For example, in a queue or a race, finishing 1st is different from finishing 2nd.
        </p>
        <p>
            <p><term>1. The Permutation Formula:</term></p>
            <line>When we want to choose <m>r</m> items from a total of <m>n</m> items and arrange them in order, we use the formula:</line>
            <me>_nP_r = P(n, r) = \frac{n!}{(n-r)!}</me>
        </p>
        <p>
           <p> <term>2. Applying the Formula</term></p>
            <line>If you are picking a Prefect and Assistant Prefect from 5 people, you are looking for <m>_5P_2</m>:</line>
            <me>_5P_2 = \frac{5!}{(5-2)!} = \frac{5!}{3!} = \frac{5 \times 4 \times 3!}{3!} = 20 \text{ ways.}</me>
        </p>
        <p>
           <p> <term>3. Simplifying Expressions:</term></p>
            <line>Permutation problems often involve dividing and subtracting factorials. Always perform the subtraction in the denominator first before expanding the numerator.</line>
        </p>
    </insight>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example>
       
        <statement>
            <p>1. There are 6 people waiting in a line at a bank. In how many different orders can they be served?</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Since all 6 people are being arranged, we are looking for <m>_6P_6</m> (which is simply <m>6!</m>):
                <me>_6P_6 = \frac{6!}{(6-6)!} = \frac{6!}{0!} = \frac{720}{1} = 720 \text{ ways.}</me>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
    
        <statement>
            <p>2. In a race with 12 runners, how many ways can the Gold, Silver, and Bronze medals be awarded?</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                We are choosing and arranging 3 runners out of 12:
                <me>_{12}P_3 = \frac{12!}{(12-3)!} = \frac{12!}{9!}</me>
                <me>12 \times 11 \times 10 = 1,320 \text{ ways.}</me>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        
        <statement>
            <p>3. Evaluate <m>_8P_4</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <me>_8P_4 = \frac{8!}{(8-4)!} = \frac{8!}{4!}</me>
                <me>8 \times 7 \times 6 \times 5 = 1,680</me>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        
        <statement>
            <p>4. Show that <m>_nP_1 = n</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Using the formula:
                <me>_nP_1 = \frac{n!}{(n-1)!} = \frac{n \times (n-1)!}{(n-1)!} = n</me>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
      
        <statement>
            <p>5. How many 3-letter "words" (even if they don't make sense) can be formed from the letters in the word <term>MATHS</term>?</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                The word "MATHS" has 5 unique letters. We want to arrange 3 of them:
                <me>_5P_3 = \frac{5!}{(5-3)!} = \frac{5 \times 4 \times 3 \times 2!}{2!} = 60 \text{ words.}</me>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />

    <exercises>
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>Calculate <m>_7P_2</m>.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p><m>\frac{7!}{5!} = 7 \times 6 = 42</m></p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>A Mathematics club at Marakusi Comprehensive School has 15 members. In how many ways can they elect a President, Secretary, and Treasurer?</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p><m>_{15}P_3 = 15 \times 14 \times 13 = 2,730</m> ways.</p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>There are 5 empty seats on a bus and 3 people board. In how many different ways can they be seated?</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p><m>_5P_3 = \frac{5!}{2!} = 5 \times 4 \times 3 = 60</m> ways.</p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>Evaluate <me>\frac{_6P_3}{3!}</me></p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <m>_6P_3 = 6 \times 5 \times 4 = 120</m>.
                    <m>120 / 6 = 20</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>If <m>_nP_2 = 56</m>, find <m>n</m>.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <m>n(n-1) = 56</m>. 
                    We look for two consecutive numbers that multiply to 56. 
                    <m>8 \times 7 = 56</m>, so <m>n = 8</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
    <title>Differentiation</title>

    <table>
        <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
        <tabular halign="left">
            <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                <cell>Learner Group</cell>
                <cell>Strategy &amp; Learning Experience</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
                <cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell>
                <cell>
                    <line>Use role cards, name tags, or counters to physically model different leadership positions and seating arrangements.</line>
                    <line>Encourage learners to list all possible arrangements for small cases involving 2 or 3 people before introducing the permutation formula.</line>
                    <line>Provide structured tables showing how the number of available choices decreases after each selection.</line>
                </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
                <cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell>
                <cell>
                    <line>The core lesson activities as described above.</line>
                    <line>Apply the permutation formula to solve routine problems involving positions, rankings, and ordered selections.</line>
                </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
                <cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell>
                <cell>
                    <line>Challenge learners to compare situations where order matters and where order does not matter, explaining the difference.</line>
                    <line>Provide reverse problems such as finding <m>n</m> when the value of <m>_nP_r</m> is known.</line>
                    <line>Ask learners to create and solve their own real-life permutation scenarios involving school leadership, sports rankings, passwords, or seating arrangements.</line>
                </cell>
            </row>

        </tabular>
    </table>
</axiom>

  <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-methods.ptx" />

 <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/reflection.ptx"/>

</section>